  "items": [
    {
      "format": "essay",
      "difficulty": "medium",
      "context": "Mei measures the rate at which an enzyme produces a product. She uses the same enzyme concentration in every trial and changes the conditions shown below. Rates are measured in micromoles of product per minute. In the last trial, Mei heats the enzyme for 10 minutes before measuring its activity at 37°C.\n\n| Assay temperature (°C) | pH | Substrate (μM) | Inhibitor | Treatment before assay | Rate (μmol/min) |\n|---:|---:|---:|---|---|---:|\n| 25 | 7 | 10 | No | None | 20 |\n| 37 | 7 | 10 | No | None | 40 |\n| 55 | 7 | 10 | No | None | 8 |\n| 37 | 4 | 10 | No | None | 6 |\n| 37 | 7 | 10 | Yes | None | 15 |\n| 37 | 7 | 100 | No | None | 50 |\n| 37 | 7 | 100 | Yes | None | 48 |\n| 37 | 7 | 10 | No | 10 minutes at 55°C | 9 |\n",
      "question": "Answer all parts of the question.\n\n(a) Identify the assay temperature that produces the highest rate at pH 7 with 10 μM substrate and no inhibitor.\n\n(b) Explain how the result for the enzyme heated at 55°C before the assay supports a claim about the effect of heat on enzyme shape.\n\n(c) Explain how changing the pH from 7 to 4 could cause the observed change in rate at 37°C.\n\n(d) Propose a mechanism for the inhibitor’s effect on enzyme activity. Justify your proposal using the results at both substrate concentrations.",
      "rubric": [
        {
          "criterion": "(a) Identifies the temperature",
          "description": "Identifies 37°C as the assay temperature with the highest rate under the specified conditions.",
          "points": 1
        },
        {
          "criterion": "(b) Uses the heat-treatment result",
          "description": "States that the previously heated enzyme remains much less active when assayed at 37°C than the enzyme that was not heated.",
          "points": 1
        },
        {
          "criterion": "(b) Explains the effect of heat on shape",
          "description": "Explains that heat can disrupt interactions maintaining the enzyme’s three-dimensional structure, altering its active site and reducing activity.",
          "points": 1
        },
        {
          "criterion": "(c) Connects pH to enzyme structure",
          "description": "Explains that a change in pH can change the charges of amino acid side chains, affecting interactions that maintain the enzyme’s shape or active site.",
          "points": 1
        },
        {
          "criterion": "(c) Connects structure to activity",
          "description": "Explains that the altered active site can reduce substrate binding or catalysis, accounting for the lower rate at pH 4.",
          "points": 1
        },
        {
          "criterion": "(d) Proposes an inhibitor mechanism",
          "description": "Proposes that the inhibitor competes with the substrate for the active site; does not claim that the data prove where the inhibitor binds.",
          "points": 1
        },
        {
          "criterion": "(d) Justifies the proposal with data",
          "description": "Notes that the inhibitor reduces the rate from 40 to 15 μmol/min at 10 μM substrate but has a much smaller effect at 100 μM substrate (50 versus 48 μmol/min), consistent with substrate outcompeting the inhibitor.",
          "points": 1
        }
      ],
      "keyPoints": [
        "At pH 7 with 10 μM substrate and no inhibitor, 37°C gives the highest measured rate.",
        "The enzyme remains less active after being heated and returned to 37°C, consistent with a heat-induced change in its three-dimensional structure and active site.",
        "A lower pH can change amino acid side-chain charges and the enzyme’s shape, reducing substrate binding or catalysis.",
        "The inhibitor’s smaller effect at higher substrate concentration is consistent with competition for the active site, though the data do not prove its binding location."
      ],
      "sampleOutline": "(a) Identify 37°C. (b) Compare the 9 μmol/min rate after heating with the 40 μmol/min rate without pretreatment at the same assay conditions; explain that heat may disrupt the enzyme’s structure and active site. (c) Explain how pH-dependent changes in side-chain charges can alter active-site shape or function and lower the rate. (d) Propose competition between substrate and inhibitor for the active site; support it with the large rate decrease at 10 μM substrate and the small decrease at 100 μM."
    }
  ]
}

